### 4. Advanced: Handling Multiple Files or Custom Logic

```java
@PostMapping("/upload")
public ResponseEntity<?> uploadFiles(
    @RequestParam(value = "files", required = false) List<MultipartFile> files
) {
    if (files == null || files.isEmpty()) {
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("No files selected");
    }

    List<String> results = files.stream()
        .map(f -> {
            if (f.isEmpty()) return null;
            try {
                f.transferTo(new File("uploads/" + f.getOriginalFilename()));
                return f.getOriginalFilename();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                return "Error: " + e.getMessage();
            }
        })
        .filter(Objects::nonNull)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

    return ResponseEntity.ok().body("Uploaded: " + results);
}
```

---

### 5. Best Practices

- ✅ Validate file size, type, and extension
- ✅ Use secure file naming (avoid `../` in filenames)
- ✅ Store files outside web root (e.g., `/uploads` not under `/static`)
- ✅ Set proper permissions on upload directory
- ✅ Consider using object storage (e.g., AWS S3, MinIO) for production

